#%PAM-1.0
#
# Lock screen authentication for "Sekai After Dark" (Quickshell).
#
# Installed to ~/.config/quickshell/pam/lockscreen and selected through PamContext's
# configDirectory.
#
# IMPORTANT: modules are listed explicitly rather than through `include`. Quickshell
# calls pam_start_confdir(), which resolves *every* config name inside the given
# directory, so `include system-login` looks for <configDirectory>/system-login,
# finds nothing, and contributes no modules at all. An auth stack with no modules
# makes pam_authenticate() return PAM_PERM_DENIED, which Quickshell reports as
# PamError (not as a wrong password), so every unlock fails even with a correct
# password. Explicit modules avoid that entirely.
#
# The auth stack mirrors the auth section of /etc/pam.d/system-auth, so lockout
# accounting through pam_faillock behaves the same as a normal login.
#
# Deliberately omitted from the login stack:
#   pam_shells   - rejects users whose shell is not in /etc/shells; irrelevant to
#                  unlocking a session the user is already running.
#   pam_nologin  - blocks new logins during shutdown; a lock screen is not a login.

auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      preauth
auth       [success=1 default=bad]     pam_unix.so          try_first_pass nullok
auth       [default=die]               pam_faillock.so      authfail
auth       required                    pam_faillock.so      authsucc

# Quickshell only calls pam_authenticate(), never pam_acct_mgmt(), so these account
# lines are inert today. They are explicit (not `include`) so they stay correct if
# account checking is ever added.
account    required                    pam_unix.so
account    required                    pam_faillock.so
